model {
  extend basalt.toolsGateway {
    metadata {
      constraint "The OAuth review registry is the one place that marks a connection as asking for its issuer's whole published scope list (scopeRequest issuer-list). Upstream preparation generates the SDK's whole-list issuers from it, each with short digests of every list reviewed since the mark (first a baseline frozen when the connection was marked, then an append-only history kept with each re-pin, last today's reviewed list), never the lists themselves, so no Worker that bundles the SDK carries the scope names; it refuses a marked connection without its baseline and two marks for one issuer however it is spelled, the source check refuses a generated list that differs, and catalog tests fail when a marked connection's requested scopes differ from its issuer's reviewed list, when a connection those tests hold to its issuer's list is not marked, or when a pinned baseline changes. A sign-in to a marked issuer that asks for exactly one of those lists records so on its grant, and renewals by releases that know the record keep it; a release that does not know it ignores it, and its grants then count only through the baseline. When a grant recorded it, or asked for exactly the baseline, and today's request is exactly one of those lists, the grant is held only to the scopes it asked for: the issuer's list growing, also while the provider still asks for the shorter list, or dropping one scope and adding another, also while a stored provider still asks for an earlier reviewed list, neither marks the account as needing new permissions nor refuses a shared grant at credential use, and the next sign-in or reconnect asks for the new scopes. Such a grant missing a scope it asked for and today's request still names is still marked and refused. Every other grant or request is held to every requested scope: other issuers, grants that asked for less (the three-scope grants from before full access, narrower apps), requests that add a scope outside the reviewed list, grants that recorded no request, first-party and client-credentials grants. So an app that adds a scope still asks its accounts to reconnect, and connecting another app through an existing grant still asks for consent when the grant lacks what that app declares. A stored provider keeps the longer scope list it once requested, so rolling the host back past a release that added a reviewed list holds grants to every scope of the newer list again. A grant stored without the whole-list flag counts as a whole-list request only when its requested scopes match the issuer's baseline list; first-party and client-credentials grants keep the scopes they asked for."
    }
  }
}
